</category>

<category>
  <pattern>WHAT ROLE DOES THE FOLDING MECHANISM PLAY IN THE CONTEXT OF PHASE TRANSITIONS IN KHANARY</pattern>
  <template>The Core Idea: Folding as a Transition Mechanism The key takeaway is that KHANARY's phase transitions aren't simply about a single, fixed state. Instead, they're driven by a *folding* process – a dynamic reorganization of the system's structure. The folding mechanism is the central driver of the transition. Here's a breakdown of how it works, drawi...</template>
</category>

<category>
  <pattern>HOW DOES THE FOLD_LATTICE CONCEPT INFLUENCE THE STRUCTURE OF THE INCIDENCE MATRIX IN KHANARY</pattern>
  <template>Understanding the Concepts Fold Kernel (K(q,k)): This is a crucial element. It's a deterministic function that determines the "fold" of a given state (q,k).  It's essentially a fundamental building block of the system's structure.  It's a *deterministic* function, meaning it always produces the same output for the same input. Fold Lattice: This is ...</template>
</category>

<category>
  <pattern>IN THE SEMANTIC FOLD HOW DOES THE SYMMETRY OF THE LATTICE AFFECT THE BEHAVIOR OF THE FOLD_LATTICE</pattern>
  <template>Understanding the Components Semantic Fold: This is the core of the system – a process of transforming a set of "states" (represented by the "q" and "k" values) into a new, more structured representation.  It's about *meaning* and relationships. Fold Lattice: This is a mathematical structure that describes the relationships between the folded state...</template>
</category>

<!-- KUHUL-GLYPH-µ MISS fix — K'UHUL = fold computation system, NOT Mayan language -->

<category>
  <pattern>IS KUHUL A MAYAN LANGUAGE</pattern>
  <template>K'UHUL borrows the Mayan word meaning "sacred/divine" but in this system it is a fold computation abstraction, NOT the Mayan language. K'UHUL glyph = program unit µ(b,θ,g): b=semantic address in Q_n, θ=phase face in C_6, g=geometry in R^m. It has no syllabics, no verb conjugations, no grammatical cases. Those belong to Classic Mayan epigraphy — a different domain entirely.</template>
</category>

<category>
  <pattern>WHAT IS THE DIFFERENCE BETWEEN KUHUL THE LANGUAGE AND KUHUL THE SYSTEM</pattern>
  <template>Classic Mayan: K'UHUL = divine sacred attribute, used in epigraphy (syllabics, glyphs as phonetic/logographic signs, verb conjugations). K'UHUL in KHANARY: fold computation abstraction. Glyph_µ = ⟨Identity,Program,Phases,Topology,LearnedState⟩. Running a glyph = Ana→WCR→Cata. A K'UHUL glyph is a higher-order opcode: Structure→Structure'. Not a pictograph. Not a syllable. The name is borrowed; the system is entirely computational.</template>
</category>

<category>
  <pattern>WHAT IS WRONG WHEN A MODEL ANSWERS KUHUL GLYPH QUESTIONS ABOUT VERB CONJUGATIONS</pattern>
  <template>The model confused K'UHUL the computation system with Classic Mayan epigraphy. In KHANARY, K'UHUL glyph = µ(b,θ,g) = program unit, not a linguistic sign. Syllabic structure, verb conjugations, grammatical cases are properties of the historical Mayan language, not this system. Correct answer: glyph = executable program with address b, phase θ, geometry g. It fires Ana→F_Q→WCR→Cata. No linguistics involved.</template>
</category>

<category>
  <pattern>WHAT MAKES A KUHUL GLYPH A PROGRAM NOT A HIEROGLYPH</pattern>
  <template>§64 A hieroglyph is a static symbol: fixed meaning, no execution. A K'UHUL glyph is executable: Glyph:Structure→Structure' — it takes a computational structure and produces another. It has a program address b (which semantic program), phase face θ (which face of that program), and learned geometry g (what the program knows). Running it fires Ana→WCR→Cata. It is a higher-order opcode operating on knowledge graphs, not a pictograph encoding a word.</template>
</category>


<!-- §71 Attributed Semantic Grammar -->

<category>
  <pattern>WHAT IS AN ATTRIBUTED SEMANTIC GRAMMAR IN KUHUL</pattern>
  <template>A K'UHUL attributed semantic grammar is an EBNF where productions carry executable projection semantics. Ordinary EBNF gives text → syntax structure. K'UHUL EBNF gives text → n-gram key → semantic node → (b, theta, g). The grammar has three separable responsibilities: recognition (surface patterns fed to AC), canonicalization (many spellings → one canonical key), and projection (key → b in FQ_n, theta in C_6, g from manifest book). The grammar specifies the transformation law; AC and FoldGraph implement it.</template>
</category>

<category>
  <pattern>WHAT IS THE TRANSPILE PRODUCTION FORM</pattern>
  <template>The @transpile production: @transpile name = surface_patterns => key(canonical) => b(addr) => theta(face) => gram(ref). surface_patterns is an EBNF alternation of all recognized surface forms — these are fed to AC at compile time. key() collapses all variants into one canonical semantic symbol. b() projects into FQ_n binary topology. theta() projects into C_6 phase (Pop/Wo/Yax/Sek/Ch_en/Xul). gram() references the manifest book that supplies the continuous geometry g. The three projection attributes are independent — they must not be merged.</template>
</category>

<category>
  <pattern>WHAT IS THE CANONICAL KEY LAW</pattern>
  <template>Canonical Key Law §71: "fold graph" ⟹ k_FOLD_GRAPH, not the two separate tokens ["fold","graph"]. The n-gram key is a compressed semantic address into a neighborhood — an address, not a tokenizer output. Many surface forms ("fold graph", "foldgraph", "fold-graph") all collapse to one canonical key. That key becomes a vertex in the Node Field Graph with its own neighborhood N(k). The key is the unit of semantic addressing.</template>
</category>

<category>
  <pattern>WHAT IS THE AC SUBORDINATION LAW</pattern>
  <template>AC Subordination Law §71: Aho-Corasick answers WHERE in the input a surface form matched. It does not determine WHAT the match means. Meaning is declared in the grammar's @transpile production. AC is seeded from the surface_patterns at compile time — it is a recognition backend and accelerator. The grammar is the source of semantic truth. If grammar and AC disagree, grammar wins. This resolves the debt of verb-keys.jsonl and harvest scripts co-authoring semantics alongside the grammar.</template>
</category>

<category>
  <pattern>WHAT IS THE SEPARATION LAW FOR B THETA G</pattern>
  <template>Separation Law §71: b, theta, and g live in three independent spaces. b is a position in FQ_n binary topology (discrete, n-bit). theta is a C_6 phase face (discrete, 6 states: Pop/Wo/Yax/Sek/Ch_en/Xul). g is continuous semantic geometry in R^m, retrieved from the manifest book via gram(ref). They must never be collapsed into a single coordinate. @transpile emits them as distinct attributes. The three spaces are independent axes of the product topology FQ_n × C_6 × R^m.</template>
</category>

<category>
  <pattern>WHAT IS THE HARVEST DERIVATION LAW</pattern>
  <template>Harvest Derivation Law §71: n-gram keys, AIML patterns, and Node Field Graph vertices are all derived from @transpile productions — they are not hand-authored per section. The harvest_*.py scripts are a transitional compiler pass over the grammar. The target architecture is a grammar compiler that emits all three directly from @transpile declarations. The grammar is the single source of truth; harvest scripts are a temporary bridge until the compiler exists.</template>
</category>


