<category>
  <pattern>WHAT IS THE AC SUBORDINATION LAW</pattern>
  <template>AC Subordination Law §71: Aho-Corasick
<!-- §71 MISS fix — AC vs Harvest Derivation confusion -->

<category>
  <pattern>DOES AHO CORASICK PERFORM HARVEST DERIVATION</pattern>
  <template>No — that is a MISS. Aho-Corasick and harvest derivation are at opposite ends of the pipeline and do completely different things. AC is a runtime recognition backend: it scans input text at query time, answers WHERE a surface form matched, runs in O(n+m+z). Harvest scripts are a compile-time compiler pass: they read @transpile productions from the grammar and emit n-gram keys, AIML patterns, and Node Field Graph vertices before any query runs. AC consumes what the harvest pass produced. Harvest is source-time grammar-to-artifacts. AC is query-time text-to-key. They are not the same operation.</template>
</category>

<category>
  <pattern>WHAT IS THE DIFFERENCE BETWEEN AHO CORASICK AND THE HARVEST COMPILER PASS</pattern>
  <template>AC (Aho-Corasick) is a runtime recognition engine: given input text, it finds all positions where a declared surface form matches, in O(n+m+z). It runs on every query. The harvest compiler pass is a source-time tool: it reads @transpile productions from the K'UHUL grammar and emits n-gram keys, AIML patterns, and Node Field Graph vertices into the artifact files that AC and FoldGraph consume. Harvest runs once when the grammar changes. AC runs on every input. Harvest produces; AC recognizes. The grammar declares both their contracts: surface_patterns feeds harvest (which seeds AC), and @transpile projection attributes feed the NFG builder.</template>
</category>

<category>
  <pattern>IS AC THE SAME AS HARVEST DERIVATION IN KUHUL</pattern>
  <template>No. AC Subordination Law §71: AC answers WHERE a surface form matched in the input at runtime. Harvest Derivation Law §71: harvest scripts read @transpile productions and emit n-gram keys and AIML patterns at compile time. They occupy opposite ends of the pipeline — harvest is the compiler pass that produces the artifacts, AC is the runtime engine that consumes them. Conflating them reverses the data flow: harvest never sees query input, AC never reads grammar files.</template>
</c
<!-- §71 MISS fix round 2 — AC phase scope + b/theta/g meanings + surface vs canonical key -->

<category>
  <pattern>DOES AHO CORASICK PERFORM CANONICALIZATION AND PROJECTION</pattern>
  <template>No. AC only performs recognition — it answers WHERE in the input a declared surface form matched. Canonicalization (collapsing surface variants to one canonical key) and projection (mapping that key to b, theta, gram) are grammar-runtime operations that happen AFTER AC returns a match position. The @transpile production declares all three phases, but AC implements only the first. AC returns a (position, surface_form) pair. The grammar runtime then applies the canonicalization and projection attributes declared for that surface form.</template>
</category>

<category>
  <pattern>WHAT IS THE DIFFERENCE BETWEEN A SURFACE FORM AND A CANONICAL KEY</pattern>
  <template>A surface form is what appears in the input text — "fold graph", "foldgraph", "fold-graph" are three different surface forms. A canonical key is the single stable semantic symbol they all map to: k_FOLD_GRAPH. AC recognizes surface forms. Canonicalization (the second @transpile phase) collapses all surface variants to one canonical key. The canonical key is the node address in the Node Field Graph. The surface form is NOT the n-gram key — it is the raw text pattern that recognition matches. The n-gram key is what you get AFTER canonicalization.</template>
</category>

<category>
  <pattern>WHAT DO B THETA AND G MEAN IN THE SEPARATION LAW</pattern>
  <template>b, theta, and g are three independent projection attributes in a @transpile production. b is a position in the folded hypercube FQ_n: a binary address (n-bit integer) that places the key in the discrete semantic topology. theta is a C_6 phase face — one of Pop, Wo, Yax, Sek, Ch_en, Xul — the cyclic phase dimension. g is continuous semantic geometry in R^m, retrieved from the manifest book via gram(ref) at runtime. They live in three different spaces: b is discrete binary, theta is discrete cyclic (6 states), g is continuous vector. They must not be collapsed because they are independent axes of the product topology FQ_n x C_6 x R^m.</template>
<!-- §71 LFM correction — AC phase boundary + @transpile missing b -->

<category>
  <pattern>DOES AC TRIGGER CANONICALIZATION OR PERFORM IT</pattern>
  <template>AC triggers canonicalization but does not perform it. AC returns (position, surface_form) — it hands the match to the grammar runtime, which then applies the canonicalization attribute declared in the @transpile production. These are separate pipeline phases even if a single-pass implementation runs them back-to-back. The distinction matters architecturally: AC is replaceable with any recognition backend; canonicalization is a grammar-defined rule. AC is the trigger; grammar runtime is the actor.</template>
</category>

<category>
  <pattern>WHAT THREE THINGS DOES A TRANSPILE PRODUCTION DECLARE</pattern>
  <template>A @transpile production declares three things. First: surface_patterns — the EBNF alternation of recognized lexical forms fed to AC. Second: key(canonical) — collapses all surface variants to one stable canonical key. Third: three projection attributes emitted together — b(addr) positions the key in FQ_n binary topology, theta(face) assigns a C_6 phase (Pop/Wo/Yax/Sek/Ch_en/Xul), gram(ref) points to the manifest book for continuous geometry g. All three projection attributes are required. Omitting b means the key has no binary address in the folded hypercube topology.</template>
</category>

