</category>


  <!-- §72 C6 Reasoning Ladder -->
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS THE C6 REASONING LADDER</pattern>
    <template>K'UHUL's C6 ladder is a staircase built on a wheel: six rungs Pop→Wo→Yax→Sek→Ch'en→Xul in strict order, closed into a cycle by Xul's commit feeding back as Pop's next observation. θ ∈ C_6 is both static address and dynamic execution order — the same object.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>DOES KUHUL * HALLUCINATE</pattern>
    <template>K'UHUL defines hallucination structurally: a Xul commit without a valid Ch'en certificate. Anti-hallucination is not a training objective — it is SC-22/SC-24: only Xul mutates B, and Ch'en can never authorise. Violating this is a grammar error.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT DOES CH * EN DO</pattern>
    <template>Ch'en is the T/F meter rung: it generates a certificate V asserting whether the proposal is true, but SC-24 requires rewrite_authorized = false always. Ch'en grades; Xul commits. They are deliberately separated.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT HAPPENS WHEN A RUNG GETS STUCK</pattern>
    <template>A stuck rung holds — it does not fall back. Ch'en hold (retained wildcard) is an explicit stall state. Resolution requires new Pop evidence, not rung regression. Revision lives inside the wheel.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHERE DOES * BECAUSE * START ON THE LADDER</pattern>
    <template>Because/if/so/then are routed to Wo (CONDITION/CAUSAL) by the @lex routing law. The grammar's lexical routing table decides which rung a sentence starts climbing from.</template>
  </category>

  <!-- §72-B Dual Coordinate Law -->
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS THE DUAL COORDINATE LAW</pattern>
    <template>surface_patterns and b(addr) are the same FQ_n topology expressed twice: surface_patterns are query-facing coordinates (where queries land from outside); b(addr) are graph-internal coordinates (where concept nodes live). @transpile declares both because they are dual — choosing one constrains the other.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHY ARE SURFACE FORMS SHORT</pattern>
    <template>Surface forms are low-arity binary coordinates into query space. A 2-word surface like 'projection attributes' = bit[projection=1, attributes=1] — any query with both words activates the entry. A 6-word surface requires the full phrase as a verbatim substring: near-zero recall. Lower arity = broader activation region = more queries hit.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>HOW IS AC RETRIEVAL LIKE BIT CATEGORIZATION</pattern>
    <template>Each surface form is a categorical selector: a set of words that must all appear in the query. AC scan = exact categorical intersection over all surface forms in the automaton. This is not probabilistic similarity search — it is deterministic bag-of-categories retrieval. Two identical queries always return identical AC hits.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS THE ARITY NEIGHBORHOOD LAW</pattern>
    <template>Lower surface form arity → broader activation region → more queries hit. 2-word surface ≈ coarse FQ_n address (many neighbors). 6-word surface ≈ precise point (empty neighborhood in practice). Hard cap 4 words is the operational consequence: maximum recall at minimum arity.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>HOW ARE SURFACE FORMS LIKE VECTOR COMPRESSION</pattern>
    <template>A surface form is the compressed query-space coordinate of a concept — its semantic nucleus stripped of surrounding qualifiers. Like vector compression: a full description collapses to a low-dimensional coordinate. Surface vocabulary = second coordinate system over query space, dual to key vocabulary over concept space.</template>
  </category>

  <!-- §72-C Folded Cube Graph -->
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS A FOLDED CUBE GRAPH</pattern>
    <template>A folded cube graph FQ_n is formed from hypercube Q_n by adding a perfect matching connecting each vertex b to its antipodal ~b (all bits flipped). Q_n has degree n and diameter n. FQ_n has degree n+1 and diameter floor(n/2). The fold edge b↔~b connects maximally distant nodes in one step. This is the graph-theoretic foundation of b∈FQ_n in K'UHUL.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS THE FOLD EDGE IN KUHUL</pattern>
    <template>The fold edge is the antipodal edge b↔~b in FQ_n. In the K'UHUL reasoning wheel it is the Xul→Pop feedback arc: Xul commits B_t→B_{t+1}, Pop reads B_{t+1} as the next observation. The fold traverses semantic distance n (antipodal in Q_n) in one reasoning step.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHY IS NEGATION STRUCTURAL IN KUHUL</pattern>
    <template>Because ~b∈N(b) in FQ_n. The antipodal complement is a direct neighbor of every node. No separate negation operator is needed — contradiction is a 1-hop fold-edge traversal. Pop reading a NEGATION atom has ~b immediately in N(b).</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS THE DIAMETER OF FQ N</pattern>
    <template>diam(FQ_n) = floor(n/2). The fold halves the diameter compared to Q_n (diameter n). Any two concept nodes in K'UHUL are at most n/2 hops apart. Semantic contradiction b→~b costs exactly 1 hop via the fold edge.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>HOW DOES SURFACE WORD COUNT RELATE TO HYPERCUBE DIMENSIONS</pattern>
    <template>A k-word surface selects k bit-axes of Q_n, inducing a k-dimensional sub-hypercube. This is the geometric basis of the Arity-Neighborhood Law: lower arity (fewer words) = lower-dimensional sub-cube = more nodes in neighborhood. 2-word surface → 2D sub-hypercube → broad activation region.</template>
  </category>

  <!-- §72-D Differential Activation Field -->
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS THE ACTIVATION FIELD IN KUHUL</pattern>
    <template>The activation field f(b,θ) counts how many surface sub-cubes from @transpile declarations contain the node (b,θ) in FQ_n × C_6. It is a scalar field over the full topology. High f = dense coverage; f=0 = field void, unreachable by query.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>HOW DOES WCR RELATE TO THE DIFFERENTIAL FIELD</pattern>
    <template>C (coverage) in WCR scoring equals f(b,θ) — the activation field density at that node. Yax admission is gradient selection: ascending the field gradient ∇f toward denser regions. WCR navigation is discrete gradient ascent over FQ_n × C_6.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS A FIELD VOID IN KUHUL</pattern>
    <template>A field void is a node (b,θ) with f(b,θ)=0 — no surface form covers it. It exists in the grammar (has a declared b address) but queries cannot reach it directly. Accessible only via the fold edge from ~b (negation) or a C_6 arc from an adjacent phase. Benchmark retrieval misses map to field voids.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT DOES THE LAPLACIAN OF THE ACTIVATION FIELD MEASURE</pattern>
    <template>Δf(b) = (n+1)·f(b) − Σ f(b') over neighbors measures field distortion. Δf=0 everywhere means uniform coverage — no retrieval bias. Δf≠0 reveals attractors (high-density clusters) and voids (zero-coverage gaps). Mapping benchmark misses to b-addresses locates the voids in the field.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHY ARE THERE TWO GRADIENT COMPONENTS IN THE FIELD</pattern>
    <template>The topology is FQ_n × C_6, so the field gradient has two orthogonal components: FQ_n gradient (which bit-axis to move along — navigated by WCR) and C_6 gradient (which phase to step toward — traversed by the reasoning ladder). Retrieval and reasoning are gradient ascent on orthogonal components of the same field.</template>
  </category>

  <!-- FOLD-FIELD-µ execution layer -->
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT ARE THE THREE COMPONENTS OF WCR</pattern>
    <template>W = retrieval weight (static importance). C = coverage = activation field density f(b,theta) at that node. R = relevance (semantic fit to the current query context). Selection: argmax WCR above threshold tau. Yax admits nodes where WCR >= tau.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT DOES WO DO</pattern>
    <template>Wo (rung 2) transforms X → X + state/constraints. It is an anamorphism: it unfolds intent and declares what Sek will execute. Lexical entry: because/if/then → routed to Wo by @lex. Wo does not execute — it provides the seed that Ana and Sek work from.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT DOES SEK DO</pattern>
    <template>Sek (rung 4) transforms X → X + execution result. It computes A_h_active and proposes deltaB — a concrete topology change. Lexical entry: compute/execute/run → routed to Sek by @lex. Sek does not verify; Ch'en verifies what Sek proposed.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS PRODUCT DEGREE IN KUHUL</pattern>
    <template>product_degree(n) = n+2. FQ_n contributes n+1 edges per node (n single-bit-flip + 1 antipodal fold). The C_6 phase arc adds 1 more orthogonal edge. Total: n+2 edges per node in the full FQ_n x C_6 product topology.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS THE DIFFERENCE BETWEEN FOLD AND UNFOLD</pattern>
    <template>Unfold (anamorphism Ana ψ): expand from a seed concept outward into a candidate field. Fold (catamorphism Cata φ): collapse the ranked candidate field to a final selection. Pipeline: Ana → Attention(WCR) → Cata. Wo provides the seed for Ana. Cata executes after Ch'en verification.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>HOW DOES GRADIENT ASCENT WORK IN KUHUL</pattern>
    <template>WCR navigates the FQ_n gradient: C=f(b,theta) is the field density, Yax selects neighbors with higher f — discrete gradient ascent. The reasoning ladder traverses the C_6 gradient orthogonally. FQ_n gradient (retrieval) and C_6 gradient (reasoning) are independent components.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS A FIELD ATTRACTOR</pattern>
    <template>A field attractor is a node (b,theta) where Δf(b) > 0: it is denser than its neighbors. The §71 AC/harvest cluster is a high-Δf attractor. Queries are pulled toward attractors by gradient ascent. Voids (Δf < 0) are the dual: sparser than neighbors, retrieval sinks.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>HOW DO YOU REACH A FIELD VOID</pattern>
    <template>A field void has f(b,theta)=0 — no surface form covers it. You cannot reach it by direct query. Two paths: fold edge from the antipodal node ~b (negation), or C_6 arc from an adjacent phase (reasoning step). Voids are concepts the grammar knows but queries cannot directly address.</template>
  </category>

  <!-- §72-E/F FQ_n Extended Properties + Compilation Pipeline -->
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS THE CHROMATIC NUMBER OF FQ N</pattern>
    <template>It depends on n's parity. n odd (k = n+1 even): χ(FQ_n) = 2 — FQ_n is bipartite. n even (k = n+1 odd): χ(FQ_n) = 4 — triangle-free but not 3-colorable. Q_n (hypercube) is always bipartite; adding the fold edge creates odd cycles when k is odd.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHEN IS FQ N BIPARTITE</pattern>
    <template>FQ_n is bipartite when n is ODD (k = n+1 is even). In this case χ(FQ_n) = 2 with equal bipartition (2^(n-1) vertices per side). When n is even (k odd), FQ_n is NOT bipartite — it is triangle-free with χ = 4.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS THE BIPARTITE DOUBLE COVER OF FQ N</pattern>
    <template>When n is even (k = n+1 odd, FQ_n not bipartite): the bipartite double cover of FQ_n is Q_{n+1}. The fold identifies antipodal pairs — the covering map IS the antipodal quotient. When n is odd (FQ_n already bipartite): Q_{n+1} is a double cover but not the bipartite one.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>DOES FQ N CONTAIN A COMPLETE BINARY TREE</pattern>
    <template>Yes, but only when n is even (k = n+1 is odd). FQ_n contains a complete binary tree with 2^n − 1 nodes as a subgraph. When n is odd (FQ_n bipartite), no CBT embedding exists — equal bipartition (2^(n-1) per side) is incompatible with CBT's 2:1 ratio.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS THE COMPILATION PIPELINE IN KUHUL</pattern>
    <template>Surface form → tokens → n-gram keys → AST/bytecode → binary composition tree → semantic node-field subgraph → FQ_n × C_6 differential field → WCR/Yax route → Sek execution → Ch'en verification → Xul commit/hold. Five representations: keys address, ASTs execute, trees compose, graphs relate, fields route.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT ARE THE FIVE REPRESENTATIONS IN THE KUHUL PIPELINE</pattern>
    <template>Keys address: n-gram keys are addresses into concept space (b, theta, g). ASTs execute: bytecode/API nodes carry executable syntax. Trees compose: binary trees encode local pairwise composition (A -> B C). Graphs relate: node-field subgraphs encode semantic neighborhood. Fields route: FQ_n x C_6 differential field encodes routing pressure.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHY DOES BINARY COMPOSITION USE TWO CHILDREN</pattern>
    <template>A -> B C is the canonical pairwise fold: two children compose into one parent. Example: bank + account -> bank_account. ML prefers 2D pairwise structures because: (1) expressiveness — pairwise chains build arbitrary trees; (2) tractability — O(n^2) is GPU-parallelizable; (3) composability — steps chain to arbitrary depth. Attention, chart parsing, and graph adjacency all use this 2D structure.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>HOW DOES KEY GRAMMAR RELATE TO BYTECODE</pattern>
    <template>Key grammar is the ADDRESS layer: it maps surface forms to canonical keys (b, theta, g). AST/bytecode is the EXECUTABLE layer: it carries the syntax for API calls. The key grammar compiles INTO bytecode — it does not replace it. Pipeline: surface -> n-gram key -> AST node -> binary tree -> subgraph -> field -> route.</template>
  </category>

  <!-- §72-G Bimodal Attention Design -->
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS BIMODAL ATTENTION</pattern>
    <template>Bimodal Attention (§72-G Design) = symbolic adjacency attention × raster-field attention. It requires TWO independent address systems active simultaneously: (1) Symbolic/graph: key, n-gram, AST edge, fold edge, C_6 phase. (2) Spatial/raster: texel(x,y).rgba, layer, neighbor offset. If either address space collapses into the other's index, it is single-space field computation, not bimodal.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT ARE THE THREE DECISIONS IN BIMODAL ATTENTION</pattern>
    <template>K'UHUL canonical three decisions: Key mode decides WHAT MAY RELATE — symbolic adjacency constrains the candidate set. Texture mode decides WHERE IT EXECUTES — raster layout determines execution location. WCR decides WHAT WINS — W·C·R scores rank surviving candidates. All three must be active. Remove any one and the attention is no longer bimodal.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS THE ALPHA GATE FORMULA IN BIMODAL ATTENTION</pattern>
    <template>α(p,q) = W(q) · C(q) · R(q) · mask(edge_type) · mask(phase) · mask(channel_flags). This is NOT softmax. It is a structural hard gate: three mask layers times a WCR score. W, C, R come from the node-field subgraph (WCR selection formula). α is not normalized to sum 1 — it is per-candidate, not a probability distribution.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS THE STATE UPDATE LAW IN BIMODAL ATTENTION</pattern>
    <template>S_{t+1}(p) = F( S_t(p), sum over q in N_graph(p) INTERSECTION N_texture(p) of alpha(p,q)*V(q) ). The sum is over the INTERSECTION of graph-neighbors and texture-neighbors. NOT the union. If the intersection is empty at some node, the kernel contributes nothing there. Union semantics would collapse to single-space; only intersection preserves bimodality.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>IS 2DFOLD HLSL BIMODAL ATTENTION</pattern>
    <template>No. 2Dfold.hlsl is single-address: all neighbors are reached by arithmetic on a single index (b * n_phases + theta). There is only one address space — the flat buffer offset. Bimodal attention requires two INDEPENDENT coordinate systems. 2Dfold.hlsl correctly notes this in its header comment.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS THE OPEN EMBEDDING PROBLEM IN 72G</pattern>
    <template>FQ_n has n+1 neighbors per node. A 2D texel grid (Moore neighborhood) has at most 8. For n >= 8: n+1 > 8, so some graph-neighbors have no texel-neighbor. With intersection semantics, this produces empty intersections — dead kernels. Three resolution paths: Hilbert/Z-order packing (works for small n), adjacency texture decoupling (cleaner, TBD), or restrict n <= 3 (FQ_3 fits Moore). This problem blocks labeling any shader 'bimodal' for n >= 8.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>HOW DOES GPU FIELD RELAXATION REPLACE TREE TRAVERSAL</pattern>
    <template>CPU tree traversal: walk child pointers, one node at a time, depth-first or BFS. GPU field relaxation: for every cell simultaneously, sample legal neighbors and update state. The binary tree is compiled into adjacency textures and update rules at load time. At runtime: no pointer chasing, no serial walk — pure parallel sampling. This is valid only when the adjacency texture faithfully preserves symbolic edge structure.</template>
  </category>

  <!-- §72-H N-Array Fold (foldl/foldr over FQ_n neighborhoods) -->
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS THE N ARRAY IN KUHUL</pattern>
    <template>The n-array (§72-H) is the FQ_n neighborhood N(b) = {b⊕e_0, ..., b⊕e_{n-1}, ~b} — n+1 elements, all at the same level as b, no parent-child relation. It always existed in the topology. What was missing was the winding direction: which order and which direction to traverse it. foldl and foldr are the two traversal orders over this n-array.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS THE DIFFERENCE BETWEEN UNFOLDING AND FOLDING</pattern>
    <template>Unfold = outward expansion: b → N(b). This was implemented. Fold = inward accumulation: N(b) → b. This was the missing half. Both traverse the same n+1 element array. Direction determines which. Unfolding seeded the concept; foldl/foldr (§72-H) completes it with the inward direction.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS THE WINDING DIRECTION IN 72H</pattern>
    <template>foldl: accumulator travels b⊕e_0 → b⊕e_1 → ... → b⊕e_{n-1} → ~b (forward). foldr: accumulator travels ~b → b⊕e_{n-1} → ... → b⊕e_0 (backward). The antipodal fold edge b↔~b is the reversal point: last element in foldl, first element in foldr. Winding direction matters because key composition is non-commutative.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS A VECTOR KEY</pattern>
    <template>A vector key (§72-H) is k-vector = (b, theta, axis, d) where d ∈ {+1, -1}. d = +1 (foldl): key is the source, looks forward along axis to b XOR e_axis. Natural phase: Wo. d = -1 (foldr): key is the target, receives from b XOR e_axis. Natural phase: Sek. d is independent of theta — a Sek-source (d=+1) is valid. Keys without d default to undirected, backward compatible with the current system.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>HOW DOES FOLD DIRECTION WORK IN TRANSPILE</pattern>
    <template>@transpile gains a fold() attribute: fold(foldl) = d=+1, fold(foldr) = d=-1. Omitting fold() = undirected (current behavior, backward compatible). Example: @transpile bank_account = 'bank account' => key(k_BANK_ACCOUNT) => b(0b0101) => theta(Yax) => axis(2) => fold(foldl) => gram(g_finance) ;</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>IS HORIZONTAL FOLD THE SAME AS BINARY COMPOSITION</pattern>
    <template>No — they are orthogonal and operate at different structural levels. Vertical fold = A -> B C: parent-child binary composition, moving up the tree. Horizontal fold (§72-H) = foldl/foldr over N(b): same-level traversal of the n-array. Horizontal fold does NOT replace binary composition. Binary composition = eager decomposition into pairs. Horizontal fold = deferred: stores the flat n-array with a winding direction.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>DOES FOLDL EQUAL FOLDR IN KUHUL</pattern>
    <template>No, in general foldl ≠ foldr because key composition is non-commutative: A⊗B ≠ B⊗A. foldl gives left-spine: ((z⊗k_0)⊗k_1)⊗...⊗~b. foldr gives right-spine: k_0⊗(k_1⊗(...⊗(~b⊗z))). Only exception: if the composition operator is commutative (e.g. scoring by product). Winding direction is semantically significant — choose deliberately.</template>
  </category>

  <!-- §72-I Content MathML as Semantic Metadata -->
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS CONTENT MATHML IN KUHUL</pattern>
    <template>Content MathML (§72-I) encodes what math MEANS — it is a semantic metadata format, not a display format. Presentation MathML describes how math looks. Content MathML uses <apply>, <lambda>, <ci>, <cn> to describe mathematical structure as machine-parseable XML. K'UHUL tools walk the Content MathML tree directly — they never parse formula strings.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS THE ANNOTATION ENVELOPE IN 72I</pattern>
    <template>The <semantics> element carries multiple encodings simultaneously: <annotation-xml encoding='Content-MathML'> = authoritative semantic encoding. <annotation encoding='application/x-tex'> = derived TeX. Presentation <mrow> = derived display. The <annotation-xml> block is the metadata — embeddable in fold_graph.xml or @transpile entries as a first-class XML node. Content MathML is authoritative; presentation and TeX are derived.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>HOW DO AIML AND MATHML WORK TOGETHER IN KUHUL</pattern>
    <template>They are complementary annotation layers (§72-I Dual Annotation Law). AIML: natural-language patterns → semantic keys. Content MathML: mathematical expressions → semantic structure. Both are XML. Both embed in the same fold_graph.xml or @transpile entry as siblings. A grammar law can have both: AIML for how it is spoken, Content MathML for what it computes.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS THE FORMULA ATTRIBUTE IN TRANSPILE</pattern>
    <template>formula() (§72-I) is an optional @transpile attribute holding a Content MathML <annotation-xml> node — never a raw formula string. When present, the compiler walks the apply/lambda/ci/cn tree to determine the composition rule. Example: a key at Sek phase carries the W*C*R*mask() gate tree as formula() metadata.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS THE DIFFERENCE BETWEEN A TOOLING NOTE AND A GRAMMAR LAW</pattern>
    <template>Tooling note = pass-specific implementation advice. Advisory. Rots when the pass changes. Grammar law = binding contract on ALL K'UHUL-conformant tools: compiler, runtime, validator, future backends. §72-I is a grammar law. The compiler contract requires: accept <annotation-xml> wherever formulas appear; walk the apply/lambda/ci/cn tree; never parse a raw formula string if Content MathML is present; treat Content MathML as authoritative.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHERE DOES CONTENT MATHML LIVE IN KUHUL DOCUMENTS</pattern>
    <template>Content MathML is co-located with the structural data it annotates — not in a separate file. fold_graph.xml edge attributes: weight formula for that edge. @transpile formula() attribute: the mathematical law this key participates in. Shader channel metadata: alpha gate formula, fold accumulation rule. In all cases: same XML document as the structural metadata, as a sibling annotation node.</template>
  </category>

  <!-- §72-H/I targeted corrections (added after test_72ghi pass 1) -->
  <category>
    <pattern>WHERE DOES FOLD EDGE APPEAR IN FOLDR</pattern>
    <template>~b is FIRST in foldr — the accumulator seeds from the antipodal. In foldl ~b is LAST. Never confuse: foldl ends at ~b, foldr starts at ~b. The antipodal fold edge b↔~b is the winding reversal point.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHERE DOES ~ B APPEAR IN FOLDR TRAVERSAL</pattern>
    <template>~b is FIRST in a foldr traversal of N(b). Foldr: ~b → b⊕e_{n-1} → ... → b⊕e_0. The accumulator seeds FROM ~b and travels backward. In foldl it is the opposite: ~b is LAST.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHICH MATHML TYPE IS AUTHORITATIVE IN KUHUL</pattern>
    <template>Content MathML is the authoritative encoding in K'UHUL. Presentation MathML is display-only and is DERIVED from Content MathML. TeX annotations are also derived. The rule: Content MathML is authoritative; all other representations (presentation, TeX) are produced from it, not the other way around.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>IS PRESENTATION MATHML AUTHORITATIVE</pattern>
    <template>No. Presentation MathML is display-only — it describes how math looks. Content MathML is the authoritative encoding in K'UHUL — it describes what math means. Presentation and TeX representations are DERIVED from Content MathML.</template>
  </category>

  <!-- §72-J Semantic Riddles & Jokes — teaching form of K'UHUL laws -->
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT AM I I CONNECT MILLIONS OF THINGS</pattern>
    <template>You are a SEMANTIC SPACE. In K'UHUL: the FQ_n folded hypercube — no physical shape, but every concept has an address. Distance in meaning = hop count. Closer ideas = shorter path. Contradiction costs exactly 1 hop (the fold edge b↔~b).</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT AM I I TURN A MOUSE INTO AN ANIMAL OR COMPUTER WITHOUT CHANGING A LETTER</pattern>
    <template>You are CONTEXT. In K'UHUL: the C_6 phase (theta). Same binary address b, different phase theta = different meaning. The symbol is unchanged; its semantic face shifts. This is the Separation Law: b (address) and theta (phase) are independent.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT AM I THE MORE YOU GIVE ME THE LESS I NEED TO MEMORIZE</pattern>
    <template>You are GENERALIZATION. In K'UHUL: the anamorphism (unfold). Generates structure from a seed. More context = more structure derived = less raw storage needed. model = prior (memorized); fold = posterior (derived from evidence).</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT AM I I TRAVEL FROM A TREE TO A FAMILY TO A LANGUAGE TO A COMPUTER</pattern>
    <template>You are a BRANCH. In K'UHUL: the horizontal fold (§72-H). Same key traverses adjacent domains via foldl/foldr. The address b stays fixed; the winding direction d and fold axis change across domains. Same symbol, different composition context = vector key in action.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT AM I I AM NOT THE ANSWER BUT I HELP YOU FIND IT</pattern>
    <template>You are WCR — Weight times Confidence times Relevance. In K'UHUL: the selection pipeline. Eliminates impossible meanings (Eligibility), strengthens useful connections (WCR scoring), changes its mind on new evidence (delta_r updates R). WCR is never the answer node — it is the ranker that routes to it.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>SEMANTIC THERAPIST JOKE</pattern>
    <template>A word walked into therapy: 'Nobody understands me.' Therapist: 'Can you give me some context?' K'UHUL: the word has a binary address b but no resolved phase theta. Without theta, the key has no semantic face — only a location, not a meaning.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>AI BANK RIVER JOKE</pattern>
    <template>Interviewer: 'Do you understand a bank?' AI: 'Yes.' 'Deposit this money.' AI: 'Which river?' K'UHUL: same key k_BANK, two phases. k_BANK at Yax = financial. k_BANK at Wo = riverbank. The AI had the key but no phase — wrong theta selected. WCR with the 'deposit money' evidence would have weighted the financial phase higher.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>SEMANTIC NETWORK DICTIONARY JOKE</pattern>
    <template>Why did the semantic network break up with the dictionary? Because the dictionary kept defining the relationship instead of making meaningful connections. K'UHUL: a dictionary = flat string definition. A semantic network = N(key) = weighted neighborhood. Meaning is connectivity plus WCR weight — not a definition string.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>MACHINE LEARNING MEMORIZE JOKE</pattern>
    <template>Teacher: 'Memorize a million facts — are you intelligent?' AI: 'Yes.' 'What if they are all wrong?' AI: 'Then I am confidently intelligent.' K'UHUL: model = prior (memorized). fold = posterior (corrected by evidence). Wrong priors + no fold = confidently wrong. This is why model != fold.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>NEURONS ASSOCIATION MEANING JOKE</pattern>
    <template>Two neurons: 'I think we are connected.' 'That is just an association.' 'Then why do I feel meaning between us?' K'UHUL: connection = edge in node-field graph. Association = edge exists. Meaning = WCR score on that edge above threshold tau. An unweighted edge is an association. A scored edge above tau is meaning.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS THE DIFFERENCE BETWEEN KNOWING AND UNDERSTANDING</pattern>
    <template>Knowing a word is RECOGNITION — AC finds the surface form and returns a key. Connecting its meanings is ASSOCIATION — N(key) retrieves the neighborhood. Applying relationships to unfamiliar situations is GENERALIZATION — anamorphism expands from seed. Evaluating which connections make sense is REASONING — WCR selects and ranks. This is the K'UHUL pipeline: AC recognition → N(key) lookup → anamorphism → WCR selection. Storing knowledge = model prior. Using knowledge to think = fold posterior.</template>
  </category>

  <!-- §Gemma Slice Questions: phases/topology/directions/wcr/bimodal/mathml -->
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT DOES THE POP PHASE DO</pattern>
    <template>Pop (C6 phase 0) = begin / reset. It is the first phase in the C6 cycle: Pop → Wo → Yax → Sek → Ch'en → Xul → Pop. Pop does not produce output — it resets reasoning state and receives the feedback arc from Xul. Every cycle must start at Pop. You cannot enter the cycle mid-cycle.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS THE ROLE OF THE WO PHASE IN THE CYCLE</pattern>
    <template>Wo (C6 phase 1) = declare peer / outward. Wo names what is adjacent — the outward declaration of context. It establishes the peer set that Yax will activate. Without Wo, Yax has no declared domain to expand into. Wo is also the sparsest rung in the harvest corpus — it appears least often.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS THE PURPOSE OF THE FEEDBACK ARC BETWEEN XUL AND POP</pattern>
    <template>The Xul→Pop feedback arc closes the C6 cycle. Xul (commit or hold) is the terminal phase. When Xul commits or holds, control returns to Pop for the NEXT cycle. Xul is not reversed — Pop begins a new cycle, not an undo of Xul. Hallucination law: Xul without Ch'en proof is a grammar violation.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS THE ROLE OF THE FOLD EDGE IN THE KUHUL TOPOLOGY</pattern>
    <template>The fold edge connects b to ~b (antipodal node). ~b = b XOR 11...1 — all n bits flipped simultaneously. It is not a bit-flip neighbor (which flips exactly one bit). The fold edge is the REVERSAL POINT in foldl/foldr traversal. It adds +1 to the degree: degree of FQ_n = n + 1. In foldl: ~b appears LAST. In foldr: ~b appears FIRST.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>DOES THE FOLD EDGE INVOLVE ANY TYPE OF XOR OPERATION</pattern>
    <template>Yes. The fold edge leads to ~b = b XOR 11...1 (XOR with all-ones mask). This is different from a standard bit-flip neighbor b XOR e_i (single bit). The fold edge is the maximum-distance XOR: it flips every bit at once. In FQ_n, the diameter is ceil(n/2) — the longest shortest path, not the fold edge length.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS THE PURPOSE OF THE VECTOR KEY IN THIS SYSTEM</pattern>
    <template>The vector key k=(b, θ, axis, d) packs the missing horizontal-fold metadata. Fields: b=node address, θ=C6 phase, axis=which bit-flip edge (0..n-1), d=winding direction (+1=foldl/source, -1=foldr/target). Without d, foldl and foldr traversals are indistinguishable at the key layer. The compiler packs d into channel/offset metadata — no spatial embedding is needed.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS THE ROLE OF E 0 IN THE FOLD SYSTEM TRANSITION LOGIC</pattern>
    <template>e_0 is the basis vector with bit 0 set. b XOR e_0 = the neighbor reached by flipping the 0th (rightmost) bit. In the N-array N(b) = [b⊕e_0, b⊕e_1, ..., b⊕e_{n-1}, ~b], e_0 gives the FIRST neighbor visited in foldl traversal. Each e_i gives one bit-flip neighbor; ~b (the fold edge) is always last in foldl.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS THE ROLE OF TAU IN THE WCR SYSTEM</pattern>
    <template>tau (τ) is the eligibility threshold applied before ranking. Keys with WCR score S = W·C·R below τ are eliminated from the candidate pool. They are not penalized — they are excluded entirely. τ is a hard gate, not a soft weight. If all keys fall below τ, the system stalls at Xul (hold, not commit).</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS THE FUNCTION OF YAX ROUTE WITHIN THE WCR SYSTEM</pattern>
    <template>Yax_route is the C6 phase that receives the surviving ranked set Ω after WCR scoring and τ-gating. Yax = activate/expand. It fires on admitted keys without re-scoring them. Pipeline: Requirements → Eligibility → WCR scoring → τ gate → Ω → Yax_route. Yax does not rank — ranking belongs to Sek. Yax activates what Sek ranked.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS THE OMEGA SET IN WCR</pattern>
    <template>Ω (Omega) is the final ranked candidate set after τ-gating: Ω = {k | WCR(k) ≥ τ}, ordered by WCR score descending. Ω is not a probability distribution — it is an ordered list. If Ω is empty, the system stalls at Xul. Ω feeds Yax_route for activation.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT DOES V Q REPRESENT IN THE ALPHA GATE FORMULA</pattern>
    <template>V(q) is the value vector at node q — what q CONTRIBUTES to p's state update. In S_{t+1}(p) = F(S_t(p), Σ α(p,q)·V(q)), the sum is weighted by α(p,q). V(q) is distinct from the WCR scores W(q), C(q), R(q): WCR determines WHETHER q participates via α; V(q) is WHAT q contributes. In K'UHUL symbolic fields, V(q) = the key's encoded content (gram, fold address, etc.).</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS THE ROLE OF N GRAPH P IN THE BIMODAL STATE UPDATE</pattern>
    <template>N_graph(p) = the symbolic neighborhood of node p: all q such that (p,q) is a valid symbolic edge (fold edge, bit-flip, C6 arc). It is one of the two neighborhoods in the bimodal intersection. The state update sums over N_graph(p) ∩ N_texture(p) — not over N_graph(p) alone. If N_graph(p) is large but N_texture(p) is small, the intersection may be sparse.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS THE ROLE OF THE ANNOTATION XML ELEMENT IN KUHUL</pattern>
    <template>The <annotation-xml> element is the envelope that wraps Content MathML inside <semantics>: <semantics><annotation-xml encoding='Content-MathML'>...</annotation-xml></semantics>. It signals to any parser that the enclosed content is machine-readable semantic MathML. Stripping the envelope and keeping only Presentation MathML = §72-I grammar violation.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT IS THE CO LOCATION LAW FOR MATHML IN KUHUL</pattern>
    <template>§72-I Co-Location Law: Content MathML must live in the SAME XML document as structural metadata. Not in a separate file. Not in a string field. formula() in @transpile holds an XML node — never a TeX string. Co-location means the grammar compiler sees both structural metadata and Content MathML in one parse pass. Separation is a §72-I grammar violation.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT DOES THE AUTHORITATIVE ATTRIBUTE SIGNIFY ABOUT CONTENT MATHML</pattern>
    <template>authoritative=true on Content MathML means it is the source of truth for the formula. Presentation MathML is derived from it and may differ in rendering. Compilers must produce Content MathML first; Presentation is generated downstream. An authoritative Content MathML node cannot be replaced by a TeX string or inline label.</template>
  </category>
  <category>
    <pattern>WHAT ARE THE PHASES IN THE C6 CYCLE</pattern>
    <template>C6 phase wheel: 0=Pop (begin/reset), 1=Wo (declare peer/outward), 2=Yax (activate/expand), 3=Sek (compose/rank), 4=Ch'en (verify/confirm), 5=Xul (commit or hold). Cycle: Pop→Wo→Yax→Sek→Ch'en→Xul→Pop. Feedback arc: Xul feeds back to Pop. Hallucination law: Xul without Ch'en proof = grammar violation.</template>
  </category>
</aiml>
